@@s001 How the oral exam works
@@s002 <span>Exam</span>
@@s003 How the oral exam works
@@s004 An individual oral of <b>30 minutes</b>. You roll the die, one of the <b>12 questions</b> comes out, you open with a presentation of 5–6 minutes, then there is discussion. You bring your notebooks, but the code is not run: what counts is how you explain the theory behind your exercises. Source: the course document "Exam Preparation" (July 2026, "tentative until mid-December").
@@s005 On one screen
@@s006 Type
@@s007 individual oral, variant B22 (no preparation time)
@@s008 Duration
@@s009 <b>up to 30 minutes</b>, including the decision on the grade
@@s010 When
@@s011 one of the days 11–15 and 18–19 January 2027, 9–21. The schedule with your slot comes out <b>before Christmas</b>
@@s012 Grade
@@s013 Danish 7-point scale, external examiner
@@s014 Question
@@s015 one out of 12, drawn at random (for example with a die), plus a focus point
@@s016 What to bring
@@s017 your <b>Jupyter notebooks</b> with results and examples; you can add a short outline in the notebook
@@s018 Code
@@s019 you show results and graphs, but you <b>do not run</b> code unless you are asked
@@s020 Forbidden
@@s021 generative AI at the exam, in any form
@@s022 Admission
@@s023 all the <a href="obbligatori.html">compulsory activities</a>; otherwise grade NA and one attempt used up
@@s024 The 30 minutes
@@s025 Minutes
@@s026 Phase
@@s027 What happens
@@s028 before
@@s029 arrival
@@s030 outside the room at least <b>60 minutes before</b> your slot (times can shift during the day)
@@s031 draw
@@s032 one of the 12 questions; often also one of its focus points
@@s033 presentation
@@s034 5–6 minutes of your own: purpose, theory, method, results, interpretation. Usually they let you finish
@@s035 discussion
@@s036 they go deeper into the focus point, then can widen to the question and the rest of the programme
@@s037 grade
@@s038 you leave with your computer and bag, they decide, you come back for the grade and a short feedback
@@s039 How they assess you
@@s040 Criterion
@@s041 What they want to see
@@s042 <b>Accuracy</b>
@@s043 terminology, models and mathematical concepts used correctly
@@s044 <b>Understanding</b>
@@s045 why a method works, how it connects to the theory, what role the formulas and the central assumptions have
@@s046 <b>Application</b>
@@s047 connecting the theory to the exercise, the data, the methods and the results; how it would be used in practice
@@s048 <b>Reflection</b>
@@s049 interpretation, limits, uncertainty, model choices, alternatives: not only repeating definitions or graphs
@@s050 Grade
@@s051 Description in the exam document
@@s052 02 (sufficient)
@@s053 substantially correct account of the topic; explains the main concepts and results, with some weaknesses
@@s054 in addition, clearly connects exercise, models and theory, with solid reasoning and interpretation
@@s055 precise, well organised, theoretically informed, critical; goes beyond description towards autonomous analysis and evaluation
@@s056 What does not count
@@s057 "You are not assessed on your ability to recite implementation details or describe code line by line. Formal proofs are not required", but a clear intuition of the mathematical role of methods and concepts is needed. They also assess the ability to <b>choose</b> the most relevant parts of the programme for the question.
@@s058 The 5–6 minute presentation
@@s059 An outline that covers the four criteria
@@s060 "We want to map pupil positions to screen coordinates."
@@s061 Write it and explain every symbol: parameters, input, output, why it is linear in the parameters.
@@s062 Design matrix, least squares, how much data is needed.
@@s063 Already computed, to be shown: no code is run.
@@s064 It is the "Reflection" part that separates the high grades from the others.
@@s065 The document's advice
@@s066 "We strongly recommend preparing for every exam question": a short presentation practised for each of the 12, and the recommended exercises used as a concrete starting point. The list with the links to the atlas: <a href="domande.html">the 12 questions</a>.
@@s067 Changes of date
@@s068 After the schedule is published (before Christmas) you can change slot only through a swap with another student, notifying Dan by email with everyone in copy. Illness or impediments: notify Dan as soon as possible. On the morning of the exam the updated time must be checked again.
